WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.333
A Transferable Variant Signal That Grocery
Retrieval Cannot Reach.

00:00:04.793 --> 00:00:09.037
At a camera-based checkout, a shopper takes
one product off the shelf.

00:00:09.037 --> 00:00:11.470
Which catalog entry belongs on the receipt?

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.699
Matching a store photograph to a catalog
description

00:00:14.699 --> 00:00:16.885
is called multimodal product retrieval.

00:00:17.345 --> 00:00:21.089
Frozen encoders turn photos and descriptions
into vectors.

00:00:21.089 --> 00:00:22.529
The closest direction wins.

00:00:22.739 --> 00:00:24.333
This is tomato soup.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:26.133
Chicken noodle comes first.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:30.206
Was the flavor missing from the image
features, or lost in the match to text?

00:00:30.666 --> 00:00:32.660
With the image encoder frozen,

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.910
fit a linear classifier to labeled photos of
variants within one brand.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.303
Only its weights change.

00:00:38.513 --> 00:00:41.207
Test it on different photos of those variants.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:45.982
It reaches 97.1 percent balanced accuracy on
seen brands.

00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:47.537
Each variant counts equally.

00:00:47.747 --> 00:00:49.991
The probe needs that brand's labels.

00:00:49.991 --> 00:00:51.651
What can carry over without refitting?

00:00:52.111 --> 00:00:55.459
Average the normalized photo vectors for each
product.

00:00:55.459 --> 00:00:58.959
Within each brand, subtract the mean of its
product averages.

00:00:59.169 --> 00:01:02.613
Each arrow now shows one variant's departure
from its brand.

00:01:02.613 --> 00:01:04.338
Stack them as rows.

00:01:04.338 --> 00:01:07.276
The columns are the frozen image feature
coordinates.

00:01:07.736 --> 00:01:10.915
Split that matrix with a singular value
decomposition.

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:14.972
U gives the signed pattern across variants for
each direction.

00:01:14.972 --> 00:01:16.847
Sigma gives its strength.

00:01:16.847 --> 00:01:21.173
The rows of V transpose give the directions in
the original feature space.

00:01:21.383 --> 00:01:23.601
We keep the strongest 64 directions.

00:01:24.061 --> 00:01:27.002
Starbucks contributed no photos to this fit.

00:01:27.002 --> 00:01:28.627
Using those same axes,

00:01:28.627 --> 00:01:33.427
project its photos and the catalog texts,
normalize, and score by cosine.

00:01:33.637 --> 00:01:35.831
For these ten Sumatra photos,

00:01:35.831 --> 00:01:39.525
the mean projected photo points closest to the
Sumatra description.

00:01:39.985 --> 00:01:42.575
For a control, shuffle labels among photos

00:01:42.575 --> 00:01:45.925
within each training brand, keeping group
sizes fixed.

00:01:45.925 --> 00:01:47.089
Fit the axes again.

00:01:47.299 --> 00:01:49.843
Project the same Sumatra photos again.

00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.416
Now Pike Place comes first.

00:01:51.876 --> 00:01:54.570
We held out different brands on ten splits.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:02:00.645
For SigLIP2, the gain over shuffled labels was
13.6 points across the catalog,

00:02:00.645 --> 00:02:02.927
and 3.9 once the brand was given.

00:02:03.137 --> 00:02:06.131
We learned these directions from differences
within a brand.

00:02:06.131 --> 00:02:09.231
On new brands, they mostly helped choose the
brand.

00:02:09.231 --> 00:02:11.158
The full embedding still ranks best.
